re below, lines m and n are cut by transversal line t. The 8 angles at the intersections of these lines are labeled. Which of the following statements, when it is true, CANNOT always be used to prove that lines m and n are parallel?\n[Figure: horizontal lines n (top) and m (bottom) cut by transversal t; at line n, angles numbered 1 (upper left), 2 (upper right), 3 (lower left), 4 (lower right); at line m, 5 (upper left), 6 (upper right), 7 (lower left), 8 (lower right).]", "choices": [{"key": "F", "text": "∠1 ≅ ∠5"}, {"key": "G", "text": "∠1 ≅ ∠7"}, {"key": "H", "text": "∠1 ≅ ∠8"}, {"key": "J", "text": "∠2 ≅ ∠7"}, {"key": "K", "text": "∠4 ≅ ∠5"}], "correct": "G", "explanation": "Source-keyed answer from book_t5.md (T5-28)."}, "fields": {"stem": "Lines m and n are cut by transversal t, with angles numbered as shown. Which statement, when true, cannot always be used to prove that m and n are parallel?", "assets_patch": {"figure_required": false, "source_verification_date": "2026-09-29", "figure_reconstruction": "2026-09-29-from-explicit-givens", "reconstruction_basis": "Only geometry/labels explicitly present in the source transcription; no missing numeric data inferred."}}, "figure_from_id": "fefc225a-9f1b-56b5-903b-063c8e5d7fce"}, {"id": "c6cfcc18-dae1-504d-916c-71be7f903eb2", "source": "Existing complete source transcription T6-13; mathematical reconstruction, not a recovered original scan", "review_note": "Rebuilt a precise diagram from complete explicit givens. Independently verified the answer and each alternative; replaced source-key-only explanation.", "explanation": "The triangles share angle A. Since BCED is cyclic, the interior angles DBC and DEC are supplementary; angle ABC is also supplementary to DBC, so angle ABC=angle AED. Thus triangle ABC is similar to triangle AED by AA (B), with correspondence A↔A, B↔E, C↔D. The other stated orders do not follow in general.", "correct": "B", "release": true, "before": {"stem": "[Figure: circle with inscribed quadrilateral BCED (B lower left, C lower, E right, D top); sides DB and EC extended meet at external point A below.]\nIn the above figure: Which of the following must be true?", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "△ABC ~ △ADE"}, {"key": "B", "text": "△ABC ~ △AED"}, {"key": "C", "text": "△ACB ~ △DEC"}, {"key": "D", "text": "△ACB ~ △DCE"}, {"key": "E", "text": "none of the above"}], "correct": "B", "explanation": "Source-keyed answer from book_t6.md (T6-13)."}, "fields": {"stem": "B, C, E, and D lie on a circle. The secants from external point A meet the circle in the orders A–B–D and A–C–E. Which relationship must be true?", "assets_patch": {"figure_required": false, "source_verification_date": "2026-09-29", "figure_reconstruction": "2026-09-29-from-explicit-givens", "reconstruction_basis": "Only geometry/labels explicitly present in the source transcription; no missing numeric data inferred.", "figure": "data:image/svg+xml;base64,"}}}, {"id": "08885f56-8e44-5796-9aab-df62ac5d66e9", "source": "Existing complete source transcription T6-13; mathematical reconstruction, not a recovered original scan", "review_note": "Rebuilt a precise diagram from complete explicit givens. Independently verified the answer and each alternative; replaced source-key-only explanation.", "explanation": "The triangles share angle A. Since BCED is cyclic, the interior angles DBC and DEC are supplementary; angle ABC is also supplementary to DBC, so angle ABC=angle AED. Thus triangle ABC is similar to triangle AED by AA (B), with correspondence A↔A, B↔E, C↔D. The other stated orders do not follow in general.", "correct": "B", "release": true, "before": {"stem": "[Figure: circle with inscribed quadrilateral BCED (B lower left, C lower, E right, D top); sides DB and EC extended meet at external point A below.]\nIn the above figure: Which of the following must be true?", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "△ABC ~ △ADE"}, {"key": "B", "text": "△ABC ~ △AED"}, {"key": "C", "text": "△ACB ~ △DEC"}, {"key": "D", "text": "△ACB ~ △DCE"}, {"key": "E", "text": "none of the above"}], "correct": "B", "explanation": "Source-keyed answer from book_t6.md (T6-13)."}, "fields": {"stem": "B, C, E, and D lie on a circle. The secants from external point A meet the circle in the orders A–B–D and A–C–E. Which relationship must be true?", "assets_patch": {"figure_required": false, "source_verification_date": "2026-09-29", "figure_reconstruction": "2026-09-29-from-explicit-givens", "reconstruction_basis": "Only geometry/labels explicitly present in the source transcription; no missing numeric data inferred."}}, "figure_from_id": "c6cfcc18-dae1-504d-916c-71be7f903eb2"}, {"id": "2e77ea5f-a6ec-528c-9499-16dceb6e158f", "source": "Existing complete source transcription T9-36; mathematical reconstruction, not a recovered original scan", "review_note": "Rebuilt a precise diagram from complete explicit givens. Independently verified the answer and each alternative; replaced source-key-only explanation.", "explanation": "Angles 3 and 2 lie between the parallel lines on opposite sides of the transversal. They are alternate interior angles, hence congruent (B). Angles 1 and 3 are vertical, completing the stated transitive argument.", "correct": "B", "release": true, "before": {"stem": "If two parallel lines are cut by a transversal, then corresponding angles are congruent.\nGiven k // l, transversal t cuts k and l. Prove: ∠1 ≅ ∠2.\nProof: 1. k // l (Given); 2. ∠3 ≅ ∠2 (………); 3. ∠1 ≅ ∠3 (Vertical angles are ≅); 4. ∠1 ≅ ∠2 (Transitive Prop.)\nWhat is the missing reason for statement 2?", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "If two parallel lines are cut by a transversal then corresponding angles are equal"}, {"key": "B", "text": "If two parallel lines are cut by a transversal, then alt. int. angles are ≅"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Given"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Vertical angles are ≅"}], "correct": "B", "explanation": "Source-keyed answer from book_t9.md (T9-36)."}, "fields": {"stem": "Given k∥l and transversal t, prove ∠1≅∠2. The proof is: (1) k∥l, given; (2) ∠3≅∠2, [missing reason]; (3) ∠1≅∠3, vertical angles; (4) ∠1≅∠2, transitive property. What is the missing reason for step 2?", "assets_patch": {"figure_required": false, "source_verification_date": "2026-09-29", "figure_reconstruction": "2026-09-29-from-explicit-givens", "reconstruction_basis": "Only geometry/labels explicitly present in the source transcription; no missing numeric data inferred.", "figure": "data:image/svg+xml;base64,"}}}, {"id": "ad6ac121-0921-5c75-9ca8-036b3ff57ab6", "source": "Existing complete source transcription T9-36; mathematical reconstruction, not a recovered original scan", "review_note": "Rebuilt a precise diagram from complete explicit givens. Independently verified the answer and each alternative; replaced source-key-only explanation.", "explanation": "Angles 3 and 2 lie between the parallel lines on opposite sides of the transversal. They are alternate interior angles, hence congruent (B). Angles 1 and 3 are vertical, completing the stated transitive argument.", "correct": "B", "release": true, "before": {"stem": "If two parallel lines are cut by a transversal, then corresponding angles are congruent.\nGiven k // l, transversal t cuts k and l. Prove: ∠1 ≅ ∠2.\nProof: 1. k // l (Given); 2. ∠3 ≅ ∠2 (………); 3. ∠1 ≅ ∠3 (Vertical angles are ≅); 4. ∠1 ≅ ∠2 (Transitive Prop.)\nWhat is the missing reason for statement 2?", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "If two parallel lines are cut by a transversal then corresponding angles are equal"}, {"key": "B", "text": "If two parallel lines are cut by a transversal, then alt. int. angles are ≅"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Given"}, {"key": "D", "text": "Vertical angles are ≅"}], "correct": "B", "explanation": "Source-keyed answer from book_t9.md (T9-36)."}, "fields": {"stem": "Given k∥l and transversal t, prove ∠1≅∠2. The proof is: (1) k∥l, given; (2) ∠3≅∠2, [missing reason]; (3) ∠1≅∠3, vertical angles; (4) ∠1≅∠2, transitive property. What is the missing reason for step 2?", "assets_patch": {"figure_required": false, "source_verification_date": "2026-09-29", "figure_reconstruction": "2026-09-29-from-explicit-givens", "reconstruction_basis": "Only geometry/labels explicitly present in the source transcription; no missing numeric data inferred."}}, "figure_from_id": "2e77ea5f-a6ec-528c-9499-16dceb6e158f"}, {"id": "c9508df7-5005-5e86-aebe-4ac44ec4ec02", "source": "Existing complete source transcription T9-37; mathematical reconstruction, not a recovered original scan", "review_note": "Rebuilt a precise diagram from complete explicit givens. Independently verified the answer and each alternative; replaced source-key-only explanation.", "explanation": "Angles 2 and 3 are opposite at the same intersection, so they are congruent vertical angles (A). This fact does not require the lines to be parallel. It gives ∠1≅∠3; their corresponding positions then prove k∥n.", "correct": "A", "release": true, "before": {"stem": "If two lines are cut by a transversal and alternate interior angles are congruent, then the lines are parallel.\nGiven: transversal t cuts lines k and n, ∠1 ≅ ∠2. Prove: k // n.\nProof: 1. ∠1 ≅ ∠2 (Given); 2. ∠2 ≅ ∠3 (………); 3. ∠1 ≅ ∠3 (Transitive Property); 4. k // n (If two lines are cut by a transversal and corresponding angles are ≅, then the lines are //)\nWhat is the missing reason for statement 2?", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "Vertical angles are ≅"}, {"key": "B", "text": "If two lines are parallel then interior angles are supplementary"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Given"}, {"key": "D", "text": "If two parallel lines are cut by a transversal then corresponding angles are equal"}], "correct": "A", "explanation": "Source-keyed answer from book_t9.md (T9-37)."}, "fields": {"stem": "A transversal t cuts lines k and n. Given ∠1≅∠2, prove k∥n. The proof is: (1) ∠1≅∠2, given; (2) ∠2≅∠3, [missing reason]; (3) ∠1≅∠3, transitive property; (4) k∥n, converse of the corresponding-angle theorem. What is the missing reason for step 2?", "assets_patch": {"figure_required": false, "source_verification_date": "2026-09-29", "figure_reconstruction": "2026-09-29-from-explicit-givens", "reconstruction_basis": "Only geometry/labels explicitly present in the source transcription; no missing numeric data inferred.", "figure": "data:image/svg+xml;base64,"}}}, {"id": "fc57c575-ba11-5783-8065-6e2ff0e171db", "source": "Existing complete source transcription T9-37; mathematical reconstruction, not a recovered original scan", "review_note": "Rebuilt a precise diagram from complete explicit givens. Independently verified the answer and each alternative; replaced source-key-only explanation.", "explanation": "Angles 2 and 3 are opposite at the same intersection, so they are congruent vertical angles (A). This fact does not require the lines to be parallel. It gives ∠1≅∠3; their corresponding positions then prove k∥n.", "correct": "A", "release": true, "before": {"stem": "If two lines are cut by a transversal and alternate interior angles are congruent, then the lines are parallel.\nGiven: transversal t cuts lines k and n, ∠1 ≅ ∠2. Prove: k // n.\nProof: 1. ∠1 ≅ ∠2 (Given); 2. ∠2 ≅ ∠3 (………); 3. ∠1 ≅ ∠3 (Transitive Property); 4. k // n (If two lines are cut by a transversal and corresponding angles are ≅, then the lines are //)\nWhat is the missing reason for statement 2?", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "Vertical angles are ≅"}, {"key": "B", "text": "If two lines are parallel then interior angles are supplementary"}, {"key": "C", "text": "Given"}, {"key": "D", "text": "If two parallel lines are cut by a transversal then corresponding angles are equal"}], "correct": "A", "explanation": "Source-keyed answer from book_t9.md (T9-37)."}, "fields": {"stem": "A transversal t cuts lines k and n. Given ∠1≅∠2, prove k∥n. The proof is: (1) ∠1≅∠2, given; (2) ∠2≅∠3, [missing reason]; (3) ∠1≅∠3, transitive property; (4) k∥n, converse of the corresponding-angle theorem. What is the missing reason for step 2?", "assets_patch": {"figure_required": false, "source_verification_date": "2026-09-29", "figure_reconstruction": "2026-09-29-from-explicit-givens", "reconstruction_basis": "Only geometry/labels explicitly present in the source transcription; no missing numeric data inferred."}}, "figure_from_id": "c9508df7-5005-5e86-aebe-4ac44ec4ec02"}, {"id": "1eec461a-ac38-5b24-8b9a-3e6fff511fff", "source": "Existing complete source transcription T7-43; mathematical reconstruction, not a recovered original scan", "review_note": "Rebuilt a precise diagram from complete explicit givens. Independently verified the answer and each alternative; replaced source-key-only explanation.", "explanation": "The large square has side 6 and area 36. The small square has side |4−2|=2 and area 4. The unshaded area is 36−4=32 square units (B).", "correct": "B", "release": true, "before": {"stem": "[Figure: Coordinate plane with a large square with corners at (0,0) and (6,6), and a shaded small square with corners (2,2)-labeled (2,4) and (4,2) inside.]\nIf the quadrilaterals in the figure are squares with each side parallel to the x-axis or the y-axis, what is the area of the unshaded region within the larger square?", "choices": [{"key": "A", "text": "36"}, {"key": "B", "text": "32"}, {"key": "C", "text": "24"}, {"key": "D", "text": "16"}, {"key": "E", "text": "12"}], "correct": "B", "explanation": "Source-keyed answer from book_t7.md (T7-43)."}, "fields": {"stem": "The two quadrilaterals shown are squares with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. The larger has opposite corners (0,0) and (6,6); the shaded smaller square has opposite corners (2,4) and (4,2). What is the unshaded area inside the larger square?", "assets_patch": {"figure_required": false, "source_verification_date": "2026-09-29", "figure_reconstruction": "2026-09-29-from-explicit-givens", "reconstruction_basis": "Only geometry/labels explicitly present in the source transcription; no missing numeric data inferred.", "figure": "data